==Old English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈseːon/|lang=ang}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*sehwaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*sekʷ-||to see, notice}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{m|ofs|sīa}} (West Frisian ''[[sjen]]''), Old Saxon ''[[sehan]]'' (Low German {{m|nds|sehn}}), Old Dutch {{m|odt|sian}} (Dutch ''[[zien]]''), Old High German ''[[sehan]]'' (German ''[[sehen]]''), Old Norse {{m|non|sjá}} (Danish and Swedish {{m|se|se}}, Icelandic {{m|is|sjá}}), Gothic {{m|got|𐍃𐌰𐌹𐍈𐌰𐌽|tr=saihwan}}.

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|head=sēon}}

# to [[see]]

=====Conjugation=====
{{ang-conj|type=strong|class=5|sēon|sēonne|sēo|sīehst|sīehþ|sēoþ|sēo|sēon|seah|[[sāwe]], [[sǣġe]]|seah|[[sāwon]], [[sǣgon]]|[[sāwe]], [[sǣġe]]|[[sāwen]], [[sǣġen]]|seoh|sēoþ|sēonde|(ġe)[[sewen]], (ġe)[[seġen]]}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|ang|ġesēon}}

=====Descendants=====
* Middle English: {{l|enm|seen}}
** Scots: {{l|sco|se}}, {{l|sco|sie}}
** English: {{l|en|see}}

===Etymology 2===
Akin to {{cog|goh|sīn||to be}}, {{cog|la|sum||to be}}. Ultimately from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₁es-}}.

====Verb====
{{ang-verb|head=sēon}}

# To [[be]], used primarily in reference to God
#: '' Fæder ūser, þū sē '''eart''' on heofonum, '''sī''' þīn nama gehālgod '' &ndash; Our Father, who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.

=====Conjugation=====
{{ang-conj|type=irreg|sēon|sēonne|eom|(eart)|is|sind(on), sint · (earon)|sīe|sīen|(wæs)|(wǣre)|(wæs)|(wǣron)|(wǣre)|(wǣren)|sī, sēo|''unattested''|(wesende)|''unattested''}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*sīhwaną||to strain, drip}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*seyk-||to pour, strain}}.

====Alternative forms====
